                            = succeed "," do
                              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fokker-Planck+equation"} Fokker-Planck equation
                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lax+equation"} Lax equation
                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/equality+and+equivalence+-+contents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#adjoint_equivalences Adjoint equivalences
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %li
                %a{:href => "#applications"} Applications
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#intervals_in_homotopy_theory"} Intervals in homotopy theory
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#defining_tricategories"} Defining tricategories
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#cartesian_closed_2categories"} Cartesian closed 2-categories
              %li
                %a{:href => "#in_higher_category_theory"} In higher category theory
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            %em adjoint equivalence
            is a more “coherent” or “structured” notion of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-morphism"} 2-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
            relating composites to identities are required to satisfy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence+laws"} coherence laws
            (namely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zigzag+identities"} zigzag identities
            for an
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            An
            %strong adjoint equivalence
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            is a pair of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"} adjoint functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\dashv g
            in which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+of+an+adjunction"} unit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+of+an+adjunction"} counit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon
            are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphisms"} natural isomorphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g
            constitute an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
          %p
            There is an identical definition internal to any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p We work in any 2-category. First, we observe:
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi η
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ε
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f,g,\eta,\varepsilon)
              is an adjoint equivalence, then so is
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %msup
                        %mi ε
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msup
                        %mi η
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (g,f,\varepsilon^{-1},\eta^{-1})
          %p
            Therefore, in an adjoint equivalence, each functor is both the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            of the other (i.e. it is an
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ambidextrous+adjunction"} ambidextrous adjunction
          %p The definition as given above is also redundant:
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_2 Lemma
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi η
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ε
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f,g,\eta,\varepsilon)
              is any equivalence, then it satisfies one
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zigzag+identity"} zigzag identity
              iff it satisfies the other.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Using
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} string diagram
              notation, with strings progressing up the page and 1-morphisms progressing from left to right, we can draw the data of an equivalence (omitting labels for the regions denoting objects) as follows:
