                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} =
              .property
                category:
                %a.category_link{:href => "/nlab/all_pages/svg"} svg
            %p
              Finally, if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ε
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi f
                    %mi g
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon'\colon f g \to 1
              is any other isomorphism satisfying the zigzag identities with
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi η
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ε
                    %mo ′
                    %mo =
                    %mi ε
                    %mo ′
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ε
                    %mi f
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mi η
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi ε
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mi g
                    %mi ε
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mi η
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi ε
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\varepsilon' = \varepsilon' . (\varepsilon f g) . (f \eta g) =
                    \\varepsilon . (f g \varepsilon') . (f \eta g) = \varepsilon
            %p
              using the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interchange+law"} interchange law
              and two zigzag identities. This shows uniqueness.
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories+Work"} Categories Work
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is part of an equivalence, then it is part of an adjoint equivalence. This proof is given in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
          %p
            Since adjoints are unique up to unique isomorphism when they exist, it follows that any adjunction involving one functor which is an equivalence must be an adjoint equivalence. Therefore, for a fixed morphism
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi η
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ε
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f,g,\eta,\varepsilon)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is not an equivalence) or equivalent to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+category"} terminal category
            (if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is an equivalence). In other words, it is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28-1%29-category"} (-1)-category
          %p Therefore, in any 2-category, the following data are all equivalent (i.e. form equivalent categories):
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon X\to Y
                with the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/property"} property
                of being an equivalence.
            %li
              %p
                A morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon X\to Y
                with the
                %em structure
                of a morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi Y
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\colon Y \to X
                and an isomorphism
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi η
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ≅
                        %mi g
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta\colon 1 \cong g f
                %em property
                that there exists an isomorphism
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mi g
                        %mo ≅
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f g \cong 1
            %li
              %p
                A morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                together with the structure of adjoint equivalence data
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi f
                        %mo ,
                        %mi g
                        %mo ,
                        %mi η
                        %mo ,
                        %mi ε
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f,g,\eta,\varepsilon)
          %p
            In other words, adjoint equivalences are the way to make the property of “being an equivalence” completely into “algebraic” structure. However, they are
            %em not
            equivalent to the category of the following data:
          %ul
            %li
              A morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              together with the structure of a morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\colon Y \to X
              and arbitrary isomorphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi g
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta\colon 1 \cong g f
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ε
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi f
                      %mi g
                      %mo ≅
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon\colon f g \cong 1
          %h2#applications Applications
          %h3#intervals_in_homotopy_theory Intervals in homotopy theory
          %p
            One instance of the usefulness of adjoint equivalences is that the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/walking+structure"} walking
            adjoint equivalence” 2-category is equivalent to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+object"} interval object
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2Cat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+model+structure"} canonical (Lack) model structure
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2Cat
          %h3#defining_tricategories Defining tricategories
          %p
            The original definition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tricategory"} tricategory
            by Gordon-Power-Street involved coherence 2-morphisms with the property of being equivalences in the relevant hom-bicategories. This is fine for most purposes, but for others it is insufficient, such as the following.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Since “being an equivalence” is not algebraic structure, the GPS definition of tricategory, taken literally, is not an algebraic structure. In particular, it is not
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadic+functor"} monadic
                over 3-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globular+sets"}> globular sets
                , nor is it the algebras for a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globular+operad"} globular operad
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence+theorems"} coherence theorems
                as properties of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+object"} free
                structures.
            %li
              %p
                The definition of 3-functors and higher
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transfors"} transfors
                between tricategories include data and axioms that involve composites incorporating not just the coherence equivalences, but their pseudo-inverses. Therefore, strictly speaking these definitions are not well-defined unless the definition of tricategory comes with chosen pseudo-inverses for these coherence equivalences—in which case one should certainly also choose full adjoint equivalence data in order that the space of choices be contractible.
          %p These problems are, of course, easy to remedy by simply requiring adjoint equivalence data rather than merely single equivalence morphisms. This change was first written down by Gurski.
          %h3#cartesian_closed_2categories Cartesian closed 2-categories
          %p
            In a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed category
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equalizers"} equalizers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            one can construct the “object of isomorphisms from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Iso
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi Y
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⇉
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Iso(X,Y) \to X^Y \times Y^X \;\rightrightarrows\; X^X \times Y^Y
          %p
            where the top arrow on the right side is (composition, reversed composition) and the bottom arrow factors through
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi id
                    %mo ,
                    %mi id
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi Y
                      %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (id,id)\colon 1 \to X^X \times Y^Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Iso
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Iso(X,Y)\to X^Y
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Iso
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Iso(X,Y)\to Y^X
            are monic, so that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Iso
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Iso(X,Y)
            can be regarded either as “the object of maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to Y
            which are isomorphisms” or “the object of maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y\to X
            which are isomorphisms” (or, as is most evident from its construction, “the object of pairs of maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to Y
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y\to X
            which are inverse isomorphisms”).
          %p
            In a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+2-category"} cartesian closed 2-category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Eqv
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Eqv(X,Y)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Eqv
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Eqv(X,Y)\to X^Y
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Eqv
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Eqv(X,Y)\to Y^X
            will not in general be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+morphism"} fully faithful
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Eqv
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Eqv(X,Y)
            as “the object of not-necessarily-adjoint equivalence data
            = succeed ".\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi η
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ε
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f,g,\eta,\varepsilon)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equifier"} equifier
            to construct its “subobject of adjoint equivalence data”
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi AdjEqv
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AdjEqv(X,Y)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi AdjEqv
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AdjEqv(X,Y)\to X^Y
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi AdjEqv
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AdjEqv(X,Y)\to Y^X
            will be fully faithful, so that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi AdjEqv
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AdjEqv(X,Y)
            can also be regarded as “the object of maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to Y
            which are equivalences” and dually.
          %h2#in_higher_category_theory In higher category theory
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-category"} n-category
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-category"} infinity-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
            can be refined to an adjoint equivalence. This is known to be true at least in the following cases:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                For
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gray-categories"} Gray-categories
                = succeed "\u2018s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Lack"} Steve Lack
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1001.2366"} 1001.2366
                on the
                = succeed "." do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    model structure for Gray-categories
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/model+structure+for+Gray-categories"} ?
                = succeed "." do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    adjoint 2-equivalence
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/adjoint+2-equivalence"} ?
            %li
              %p
                For
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tricategories"} tricategories
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence+theorem+for+tricategories"} coherence theorem for tricategories
                = succeed "\u2018s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nick+Gurski"} Nick Gurski
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/26/14/26-14abs.html"} Biequivalences in tricategories
            %li
              %p
                For
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+omega-categories"} strict omega-categories
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0712.0617"} 0712.0617
                on the
                = succeed "." do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    model structure for strict omega-categories
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/model+structure+for+strict+omega-categories"} ?
            %li
              %p
                For
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicategories"} quasicategories
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
                of the
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+groupoid"} interval groupoid
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+in+a+quasicategory"} equivalence in a quasicategory
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+adjoint+equivalence"} enriched adjoint equivalence
